big: Numbers
lede: A book that opens by counting everyone and closes by deciding who inherits. Between the two it records every complaint the wilderness produced, and treats some of them as rebellion and others as law.
calls:
k: Why this is a focused study
Numbers gives the Empire its census, its camp and its line between an approved petition and a criminal one. Three things, so it gets ten pages and not seventeen.
||
k: Can be promoted
If the Trials lean harder on census and registration than expected, this spread becomes a full dossier. The review box is on the close-the-spread page.
/end

@page rh="NUMBERS · 1 OF 8 · THE BOOK ON THE RECORD" m="NUM 1.1; NUM 1–10; NUM 11–21; NUM 22–36; MILGROM, NUMBERS; TKC: DOM"
# What it is
rec:
Name | English *Numbers*, from the Greek *Arithmoi* and the Latin *Numeri*, named for its two censuses. The Hebrew name, *Bemidbar*, means "in the wilderness," from the book's opening words. The two traditions name different books.
Place | Fourth book of the Torah. It carries the story from Sinai to the edge of the land and holds the material Leviticus doesn't narrate.
Structure | Three movements by place. Chapters 1–10 at Sinai: counting, camp and departure. Chapters 11–21 in the wilderness: complaint, scouts, condemnation, rebellion, death. Chapters 22–36 on the plains of Moab: Balaam, the second census and the inheritance rulings.
Genres inside | Census register, itinerary, legal ruling, narrative, poetry, oracle, and a folk tale about a talking donkey. The registers and the stories weren't written to be read the same way.
The figures | 603,550 fighting men in chapter 1 and 601,730 in chapter 26, which implies a population in the millions. CONTESTED: Whether the numbers are literal. Proposals include a different meaning for the word translated "thousand," schematic totals and later expansion. None is settled.
Neighbors | Ancient Near Eastern military rosters, land grants and boundary records cover the same administrative ground. Reading Numbers beside them shows it as a governing document as well as a story.

k: What the book is about, if it's about one thing
INTERPRETATION: Who is on the list, and what being on the list gets you. Nearly the whole generation counted in chapter 1 is dead by chapter 26, Caleb and Joshua excepted, and most of the generation that inherits wasn't counted the first time.
prompt: What I already think this book is, before reading it again | fill

@page rh="NUMBERS · 2 OF 8 · THE COUNT AND THE CAMP" m="NUM 1; NUM 2; NUM 3–4; NUM 26; LEVINE, NUMBERS; TKC: DOM"
# Counting as governance
The census counts men twenty years old and up who can go to war, by ancestral house. What it produces is a military muster, an ancestral register and a marching order. Women and children aren't counted. The Levites are registered separately, by a different rule and from a different age. INFERENCE: A register like this also served taxation, labor drafts and conscription, judging by ancient Near Eastern practice. Numbers itself doesn't say so.
tabrl:
| | First census · ch. 1 | Second census · ch. 26 |
| Where | The wilderness of Sinai, the second year out | The plains of Moab, a generation later |
| Purpose | Muster for war and order the camp | Divide the land by the size of each tribe |
| Total | 603,550 | 601,730, almost the same after forty years of death |
| Who survives | The generation that left Egypt | Of that generation, only Caleb and Joshua |
| What it proves | The people are numerous, organized and ready | INTERPRETATION: The promise outlived the people who first received it |
cols:
k: The camp · ch. 2
Twelve tribes in fixed positions on four sides, three to a side, each under its own standard, with the Levites in an inner ring around the tabernacle and Moses and Aaron at the entrance. A tribe's place in the camp is assigned, recorded and set up the same way at every stop.
||
k: What the Empire takes
POSSIBLE: The whole apparatus with the theology intact: a register that decides who exists, a place assigned by house, and the claim that the arrangement is holy. Household registration (FR 32) is Numbers 2 on Imperial letterhead.
/end
prompt: Who is counted in the Empire, who is only registered, and who is neither | fill

@page rh="NUMBERS · 3 OF 8 · MIRIAM AND AARON" m="NUM 12.1; NUM 12.2–8; NUM 12.9–16; EXOD 15 (MIRIAM THE PROPHET); TRIBLE, “BRINGING MIRIAM OUT”; TKC: MIRIAM"
# Two speak. One is marked.
Numbers 12 is usually summarized as Miriam challenging Moses. The Hebrew is more exact than that. Verse 1 uses a feminine singular verb for the speaking while naming Miriam and Aaron together, and verse 2 shifts to plural speech. Aaron is present in both verses. The punishment falls on one of them.
rec:
Who speaks | Miriam and Aaron, both named, against Moses. The grammar puts Miriam first without removing Aaron.
The stated occasion | The Cushite woman Moses had married. The text raises her and never comes back to her.
The real claim | Prophetic authority: has the LORD spoken only through Moses? The marriage is the occasion, and the claim is about jurisdiction.
The reply | A divine speech that separates how God speaks to prophets in general from how God speaks to Moses. It ranks the office and doesn't deny that others prophesy.
The punishment | Miriam's skin is struck with a scaling disease, described as white. Aaron gets no visible punishment. He pleads for her, Moses intercedes, and she's shut outside the camp seven days.
The detail nobody quotes | The people don't move on until she's brought back. The whole march waits for the woman it just expelled.
box: What the text doesn't say
It never explains why only Miriam is marked, and it doesn't rank their guilt. Interpreters have supplied reasons for centuries. Some point to the feminine verb in verse 1. Some argue that a high priest with a skin disease would have shut down the sanctuary. INTERPRETATION: The grammar is real, and what it's supposed to justify is never stated. The silence is part of the text.
/end
prompt: Where I've seen this asymmetry: in the text, in churches, in the Empire, in my own chapters | fill

@page rh="NUMBERS · 4 OF 8 · TWO PETITIONS, TWO OUTCOMES" m="NUM 16; NUM 17; NUM 27.1–11; NUM 36; JOSH 17.3–6; TKC: TR · DOM"
# Korah and the daughters
These aren't the same act with different results. They differ in who brings them, what's asked, how and where it's asked, and how the narrator frames them. Side by side, the question isn't whether Numbers approves of dissent. It's which petitions this authority is able to hear.
tabrl:
| | Korah · ch. 16 | Zelophehad's daughters · ch. 27 |
| Who | Korah the Levite, Dathan, Abiram and On of Reuben, and 250 men of standing | Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milcah and Tirzah, five sisters, each named |
| Asks for | Priestly access: the holiness of the whole congregation against Aaron's sole office | An inheritance in their father's name, since he died without sons |
| Method | An assembly against Moses and Aaron, then a censer test of authority | They stand before Moses, Eleazar, the chiefs and the whole congregation at the tent of meeting and state their case |
| Framing | The narrator calls it rebellion before the reader can weigh it | The narrator reports the petition without a verdict and lets the ruling answer it |
| Moses | Falls on his face, then proposes the test | Takes the case to the LORD instead of deciding it himself |
| Outcome | The ground opens and fire consumes the 250. Chapter 17 secures Aaron's office again with a budding staff. | Granted, and made a general law of inheritance |
| Afterward | Korah's line survives as singers, the sons of Korah, named in the Psalms headings | Chapter 36 narrows the win: they must marry within their father's tribe so the land stays put. Granted, then fenced. |
box: The question to carry, not the conclusion to print
INTERPRETATION: A petition that works through the system's own procedure, asks for a defined thing and leaves the office alone can be granted and written into law. One that contests who holds the office is answered with the ground.
/end
prompt: Which petition is Eden bringing, and does she know which one it looks like to the Temple? | fill

